f 17592186045417] [17592186176935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the following linear transformation: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, it can only be one of the following:  \n\n- the whole of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$\n\n- a line through the origin, or\n\n- a single point (vector), namely the origin $(0,0).$\n\nLet's denote the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ by $T.$ It turns out that the dimension of the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ depends on $\\text{rank}(T)$ (or equivalently on $\\text{nullity}(T)$ by the rank-nullity theorem). Indeed, we have the following:\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=2,$ then the two columns of $T$ are linearly independent. This means $T$ is invertible and therefore, $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) =  \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto  $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7d/7de193042587cb9362641cbb541477a5e4951e96d9e82f074fb0cc3a86a80d3a.svg)\n\n![](images/88/88baa47cae14d4a43d59b366c13b3c29376678edef2fe34ff505a942ef6af986.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=1,$ then there is a nonzero vector that generates the image of $\\mathbf{T}.$ In this case, the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a one-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^2$. So, it is a line passing through the origin.\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=2x\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/49/4952615b0c4ddf85cc6d60483f8c0587093c67734a320cde913644fe8fdafb55.svg)\nNotice that the points $(2,4)$ and $(1,2),$ defined by the columns of $T,$ lie on the line $y=2x.$\n\n![](images/03/0322f183c16fe82515277d10cf27a93ae753f8eadf7eeb1db4d3c5e404480b09.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=0$ then,  according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that  $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})=\\mathbf{0}$ for all $\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Bbb R^2$. So, in this case, we have that\n![](images/4a/4a0ce67cffc02e28edffb9efeedca2e21ffc7c0d8b72ccfa0566cb29f6679865.svg)\nwhich maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\{(0,0)\\},$ i.e., the set containing only the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n![](images/12/1252907a1e9767201dd326945f328f91fc493ea283b244dbfee42a7c4248d9e6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1065 8344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0ec0551e-64f7-52a7-a0e7-827e6a8944f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177088 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366753 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "292d5352-84c3-580e-88ff-42f528f9a4e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366754 #edb/ref 1080 "(0,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366754 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366754 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9f40948d-6a2c-590f-8a11-24b48ddadb86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366755 #edb/ref 1080 "y=2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366755 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366755 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f77d896-8399-547f-8c3f-2490b781e097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366756 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\frac{3}{2}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366756 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366756 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e4ca92a7-2ef3-53a1-8ddd-54bea50ff463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366757 #edb/ref 1080 "y=x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366757 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366757 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "963c7644-d7cd-502e-9404-7dfcb73af315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366758 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366758 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366758 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8765811-b618-5407-abfc-5f01d72ccdd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366760 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1ca72594-5be6-5643-b9f7-7c9ff9fc7016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366761 #edb/ref 1080 "{R}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "98ef481a-705d-5f82-9a19-92ceb01cfee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366762 #edb/ref 1080 "(0,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366762 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366762 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a54ef0a-64c9-5842-8675-a775bf4e73ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366763 #edb/ref 1080 "y=4x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366763 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366763 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8f8f571-4006-5417-916b-8b1ccb869c3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366764 #edb/ref 1080 "y=2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366764 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366764 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52efde24-9a00-5780-9f50-bdae742b41f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366765 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-4x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366765 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366765 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fccc2756-9459-5499-8770-f77a30708a59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382648 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454875 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186382648 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454876 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186382648 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454877 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186382648 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382648 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382648 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(T)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186382648 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5aa9ee11-e88d-5123-8f62-331a18227a4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382648 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398081 #edb/ref 1071 "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186398081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96dc0ffa-c035-5c4a-b42f-7a4928dfb8df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398081 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(2,-1),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-1-0}{2-0}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398082 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398082 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398082 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186398084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398082 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "39de4ad4-8ca2-5e4e-a502-b05807674875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398083 #edb/ref 1080 "{R}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398083 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/re